1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:04,425
The Orang Laut: The Sea People
Who Kept Singapore's Waters

2
00:00:04,425 --> 00:00:08,996
When Stamford Raffles and William
Farquhar came ashore at the mouth

3
00:00:09,076 --> 00:00:13,439
of the Singapore River in January
1819, the people living in the

4
00:00:13,519 --> 00:00:18,100
boats moored along the bank were
so frightened that they all fled.

5
00:00:18,100 --> 00:00:22,408
These were orang laut, the sea
people, and by most accounts they

6
00:00:22,488 --> 00:00:27,150
made up the greater part of the
island's thousand or so inhabitants.

7
00:00:27,150 --> 00:00:31,761
They had lived in these waters for
centuries before the British arrived,

8
00:00:31,841 --> 00:00:35,085
and their story runs on
long after, to families who

9
00:00:35,165 --> 00:00:39,400
were moved off Singapore's southern
islands within living memory.

10
00:00:39,400 --> 00:00:43,050
Orang laut is Malay for
"people of the sea".

11
00:00:43,050 --> 00:00:46,679
It is not the name of a
single people but a broad term

12
00:00:46,759 --> 00:00:49,702
for the many communities
who lived on boats,

13
00:00:49,782 --> 00:00:53,823
or in houses over the water,
among the islands and estuaries

14
00:00:53,903 --> 00:00:57,475
of the Riau–Johor waters
and the Straits of Malacca.

15
00:00:57,475 --> 00:01:02,525
The Portuguese called them Çelates,
from the Malay selat, "strait".

16
00:01:02,525 --> 00:01:05,832
Each group, or suku,
had its own home waters

17
00:01:05,912 --> 00:01:09,375
and its own speech,
closely related to Malay.

18
00:01:09,375 --> 00:01:11,981
Several of these groups lived

19
00:01:12,061 --> 00:01:15,025
in and around Singapore in 1819.

20
00:01:15,025 --> 00:01:18,422
The Orang Gelam lived
around the Singapore River,

21
00:01:18,502 --> 00:01:21,472
and the Orang Biduanda
Kallang lived in the

22
00:01:21,552 --> 00:01:25,100
mangrove swamps at the
mouth of the Kallang River.

23
00:01:25,100 --> 00:01:29,644
The Orang Seletar roamed the
mangroves and rivers of the island's

24
00:01:29,724 --> 00:01:33,850
northern shore and the strait
between Singapore and Johor.

25
00:01:33,850 --> 00:01:36,701
The Orang Selat, the
people of the straits,

26
00:01:36,781 --> 00:01:39,575
are associated with the southern islands.

27
00:01:39,575 --> 00:01:43,271
Raffles recorded about a
thousand inhabitants in all,

28
00:01:43,351 --> 00:01:46,762
about 200 of them Orang
Seletar, and the Biduanda

29
00:01:46,842 --> 00:01:50,475
Kallang are thought to have
been the largest group.

30
00:01:50,475 --> 00:01:53,232
For hundreds of years the sea people were

31
00:01:53,312 --> 00:01:56,150
the naval strength of the Malay kingdoms.

32
00:01:56,150 --> 00:02:00,628
Both Portuguese and Malay sources
say that when Parameswara,

33
00:02:00,708 --> 00:02:05,261
the last ruler of Singapura,
fled to the island in the 1390s,

34
00:02:05,341 --> 00:02:09,975
one of his main sources of
support was a group of sea people.

35
00:02:09,975 --> 00:02:12,959
According to the Portuguese
writer Tomé Pires,

36
00:02:13,039 --> 00:02:17,088
who wrote in Malacca in the
1510s, it was sea people who found

37
00:02:17,168 --> 00:02:20,419
the site of Malacca and
invited him to move there,

38
00:02:20,499 --> 00:02:23,682
and the great offices of
Bendahara and Laksamana,

39
00:02:23,762 --> 00:02:28,225
the chief minister and the admiral,
were held by their descendants.

40
00:02:28,225 --> 00:02:32,459
Pires found only a few of
their villages around Singapore

41
00:02:32,539 --> 00:02:36,625
in 1511 and wrote that the
channel was "nothing much".

42
00:02:36,625 --> 00:02:41,158
The sea people who had come with
Parameswara settled not on Singapore

43
00:02:41,238 --> 00:02:45,972
but on Karimun, an island at the
southern end of the Straits of Malacca,

44
00:02:46,052 --> 00:02:49,382
where a cliff carries a
Sanskrit inscription of the

45
00:02:49,462 --> 00:02:53,875
9th or 10th century that may be
the boast of a sea people's chief.

46
00:02:53,875 --> 00:02:56,524
After Malacca fell to the Portuguese,

47
00:02:56,604 --> 00:02:59,775
the sea people served
the sultans of Johor.

48
00:02:59,775 --> 00:03:02,875
Different suku had different duties.

49
00:03:02,875 --> 00:03:06,767
Some rowed and fought in the
royal fleets, some carried envoys

50
00:03:06,847 --> 00:03:10,675
and letters to foreign rulers,
some were blacksmiths who made

51
00:03:10,755 --> 00:03:15,224
swords and spears, and others gathered
sea produce, sago and agar-agar,

52
00:03:15,304 --> 00:03:18,700
or carried water and firewood
for the royal kitchens.

53
00:03:18,700 --> 00:03:22,725
In return their leaders
received titles and gifts.

54
00:03:22,725 --> 00:03:27,596
In the 17th century the chief of
the sea people of Singapore held

55
00:03:27,676 --> 00:03:32,550
the title Raja Negara Selat, king
of the country of the straits.

56
00:03:32,550 --> 00:03:36,250
The bond was with the royal line itself.

57
00:03:36,250 --> 00:03:41,178
In 1699 Sultan Mahmud Shah the
Second of Johor, the last ruler who

58
00:03:41,258 --> 00:03:46,338
could claim descent from the founder
of Singapura, was assassinated,

59
00:03:46,418 --> 00:03:51,350
and the sea people's loyalty to
the new rulers never ran as deep.

60
00:03:51,350 --> 00:03:54,320
Over the 18th century
they became scattered,

61
00:03:54,400 --> 00:03:58,350
and the Bugis took over much
of the role they had played.

62
00:03:58,350 --> 00:04:01,174
The early accounts of the sea people were

63
00:04:01,254 --> 00:04:03,875
written by outsiders, and not kindly.

64
00:04:03,875 --> 00:04:07,474
Munshi Abdullah, the
Malay scribe who came to

65
00:04:07,554 --> 00:04:12,050
Singapore in 1819, wrote
about them with open contempt.

66
00:04:12,050 --> 00:04:15,443
The historian Charles
Buckley, writing in 1902,

67
00:04:15,523 --> 00:04:20,321
described them as people who were
"born, lived and died in boats",

68
00:04:20,401 --> 00:04:23,800
a whole family often
living in a single craft.

69
00:04:23,800 --> 00:04:27,098
Abdullah also recorded
that the Orang Gelam paid

70
00:04:27,178 --> 00:04:30,547
respect to a rock near
the mouth of the Singapore

71
00:04:30,627 --> 00:04:34,075
River that was shaped like
the head of a garfish.

72
00:04:34,075 --> 00:04:37,275
The British town soon pushed them aside.

73
00:04:37,275 --> 00:04:41,079
Soon after 1819 the Orang
Gelam moved from the

74
00:04:41,159 --> 00:04:45,550
river to Pulau Brani, an
island off the south coast.

75
00:04:45,550 --> 00:04:48,612
As shipping grew on the Singapore River,

76
00:04:48,692 --> 00:04:54,267
many families moved from the river
to the Kallang between 1823 and 1843,

77
00:04:54,347 --> 00:04:57,725
and some built a
settlement at Tanjong Rhu.

78
00:04:57,725 --> 00:05:02,116
In the 1820s the Temenggong,
the Malay chief of the island,

79
00:05:02,196 --> 00:05:05,678
resettled most of the
Orang Biduanda Kallang on

80
00:05:05,758 --> 00:05:09,850
the Pulai River in Johor,
and there disaster followed.

81
00:05:09,850 --> 00:05:13,965
A smallpox epidemic around 1847 and 1848

82
00:05:14,045 --> 00:05:18,450
reduced about a hundred families to eight.

83
00:05:18,450 --> 00:05:21,532
Descendants say a few
families stayed behind

84
00:05:21,612 --> 00:05:24,911
on the Kallang into the
1930s, and the river is

85
00:05:24,991 --> 00:05:28,225
generally thought to
take its name from them.

86
00:05:28,225 --> 00:05:31,975
The Orang Seletar kept
to the northern shore.

87
00:05:31,975 --> 00:05:36,394
In the early years after
independence some still lived at the

88
00:05:36,474 --> 00:05:40,900
mouths of the Kranji and Kadut
rivers and on Seletar Island.

89
00:05:40,900 --> 00:05:45,153
As those areas were developed,
some families moved across the

90
00:05:45,233 --> 00:05:49,850
strait to Johor and others merged
into the wider Malay community.

91
00:05:49,850 --> 00:05:55,235
After separation in 1965 the Orang
Seletar who had become Malaysian

92
00:05:55,315 --> 00:06:01,515
citizens could still cross the strait
to fish in their old waters until 1987,

93
00:06:01,595 --> 00:06:06,000
when that ended after an
arrest for alleged smuggling.

94
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:09,451
Today there are nine Orang
Seletar villages on the

95
00:06:09,531 --> 00:06:13,547
southern coast of Johor, where
they fish and farm mussels,

96
00:06:13,627 --> 00:06:17,007
and the reservoir, river
and aerospace park named

97
00:06:17,087 --> 00:06:20,900
Seletar are the main traces
of them left in Singapore.

98
00:06:20,900 --> 00:06:24,394
Until the late 20th
century, many of Singapore's

99
00:06:24,474 --> 00:06:27,675
southern islands had
villages of their own.

100
00:06:27,675 --> 00:06:32,225
Their people called themselves
orang pulau, the island people.

101
00:06:32,225 --> 00:06:35,718
Some had roots among the
sea people, and many had

102
00:06:35,798 --> 00:06:39,225
relatives across the
water in the Riau Islands.

103
00:06:39,225 --> 00:06:42,529
They fished the reefs,
built their houses on stilts

104
00:06:42,609 --> 00:06:46,125
over the water, and sold
their catch on the mainland.

105
00:06:46,125 --> 00:06:50,058
Pulau Semakau, about 8
kilometres south of the mainland,

106
00:06:50,138 --> 00:06:54,287
had a Malay village and a small
Chinese one, with a school,

107
00:06:54,367 --> 00:06:57,950
a mosque, a community
centre and a football field.

108
00:06:57,950 --> 00:07:01,025
About 600 people lived there.

109
00:07:01,025 --> 00:07:05,325
On the 29th of July 1975
Parliament approved a plan to

110
00:07:05,405 --> 00:07:09,137
reclaim the island for
a petrochemical complex,

111
00:07:09,217 --> 00:07:14,084
and most of the islanders were
moved to the mainland by 1977,

112
00:07:14,164 --> 00:07:18,625
many of them to flats at
Telok Blangah and Bukit Merah.

113
00:07:18,625 --> 00:07:22,275
Neighbouring Pulau
Seking held out longest.

114
00:07:22,275 --> 00:07:26,911
It was the last village community
on the southern islands,

115
00:07:26,991 --> 00:07:31,300
and the last families left
on the 24th of April 1994.

116
00:07:31,300 --> 00:07:34,259
Reclamation then joined the two islands,

117
00:07:34,339 --> 00:07:38,667
and the Semakau Landfill, which
takes most of the ash from

118
00:07:38,747 --> 00:07:42,850
Singapore's incineration
plants, opened there in 1999.

119
00:07:42,850 --> 00:07:47,025
Former islanders have spoken
about what the move meant.

120
00:07:47,025 --> 00:07:50,757
"Life on the island may
seem tough," Rohani Rani,

121
00:07:50,837 --> 00:07:55,350
who grew up on Pulau Semakau,
told an interviewer in 2020.

122
00:07:55,350 --> 00:07:59,075
"Yes, we were poor and
our life wasn't grand.

123
00:07:59,075 --> 00:08:02,000
But my heart was always at ease."

124
00:08:02,000 --> 00:08:05,453
Teo Yen Teck, who ran the
only Chinese shop on Pulau

125
00:08:05,533 --> 00:08:09,258
Seking with his brother and
was among the last to leave,

126
00:08:09,338 --> 00:08:13,075
put it more simply: "On the
island, we were more free."

127
00:08:13,075 --> 00:08:17,325
The descendants have begun
to tell the story themselves.

128
00:08:17,325 --> 00:08:20,847
Orang Laut SG, started
in 2020 by Firdaus Sani,

129
00:08:20,927 --> 00:08:25,598
a fourth-generation descendant
of the Pulau Semakau islanders,

130
00:08:25,678 --> 00:08:31,116
records the memories of former
islanders, gathers families for reunions,

131
00:08:31,196 --> 00:08:34,875
and sells the island food
his family grew up on.

132
00:08:34,875 --> 00:08:39,556
The Orang Seletar of Johor have
welcomed researchers and writers,

133
00:08:39,636 --> 00:08:44,757
and in 2019 a group of them visited
Singapore for a play based on their

134
00:08:44,837 --> 00:08:49,525
interviews and went to see the
places their ancestors had known.

135
00:08:49,525 --> 00:08:53,991
The National Library and the National
Heritage Board have published

136
00:08:54,071 --> 00:08:57,656
accounts of the sea people,
and some of the old island

137
00:08:57,736 --> 00:09:02,350
communities are remembered in
exhibitions and oral history projects.

138
00:09:02,350 --> 00:09:06,345
Why it matters today: Singapore's
story is usually told as

139
00:09:06,425 --> 00:09:09,998
one of migrants who arrived
by sea and built a city,

140
00:09:10,078 --> 00:09:14,925
but the people who were here first
were themselves people of the sea.

141
00:09:14,925 --> 00:09:18,381
For centuries they guarded
the straits for Singapura,

142
00:09:18,461 --> 00:09:21,317
Melaka and Johor, and
they were the majority

143
00:09:21,397 --> 00:09:24,400
of the island's people
when the British came.

144
00:09:24,400 --> 00:09:29,292
Within two generations of 1819
most of them had moved away,

145
00:09:29,372 --> 00:09:35,529
died out or been absorbed, and the
last island villages went in the 1970s,

146
00:09:35,609 --> 00:09:40,750
1980s and 1990s as the southern
islands were put to new uses.

147
00:09:40,750 --> 00:09:44,062
Much of what is known about
them today comes from the

148
00:09:44,142 --> 00:09:47,389
families who remember, and
the efforts now under way

149
00:09:47,469 --> 00:09:50,845
to record those memories are
some of the few ways left

150
00:09:50,925 --> 00:09:54,125
to keep the island's oldest
story from being lost.
